Installation Instructions for fldigi
************************************           Revised 20 September 2026

Build Requirements
==================

fldigi requires:

  * A C++11-capable C++ compiler.

  * Version 1.4 or later of the Fast Light Tool Kit (FLTK), including
    its development libraries and headers.  FLTK multi-threading support
    is required.

  * The samplerate (Secret Rabbit Code) library.

  * The PNG library.

  * The libxft library on systems using X11.

  * The libudev development library on Linux.

Additional libraries may be required for optional fldigi features.

fldigi supports both Autotools and CMake build systems.  The source tree
contains build scripts for the normal Linux build and for cross-compiling
a 64-bit Windows version using MXE.


Autotools Build
===============

The supplied build-autotools.sh script performs an out-of-tree Autotools
build.

For a native Linux build:

    ./build-autotools.sh linux

To remove the previous build directory and perform a clean build:

    ./build-autotools.sh linux --clean

The resulting build is placed in:

    build/autotools-linux

For a 64-bit Windows cross-build using MXE:

    ./build-autotools.sh mxe

or, for a clean build:

    ./build-autotools.sh mxe --clean

The MXE build uses the x86_64-w64-mingw32.static target and also creates
the NSIS Windows installer.


CMake Build
===========

The supplied build-cmake.sh script provides the corresponding CMake
build.

For a native Linux build:

    ./build-cmake.sh linux

For a clean native Linux build:

    ./build-cmake.sh linux --clean

The resulting build is placed in:

    build/cmake-linux

For a 64-bit Windows cross-build using MXE:

    ./build-cmake.sh mxe

or:

    ./build-cmake.sh mxe --clean

The resulting build is placed in:

    build/cmake-mxe

The MXE CMake build also creates the NSIS Windows installer.


Manual Autotools Build
======================

Developers who prefer to invoke Autotools directly may use the normal
Autotools procedure.  An out-of-tree build is recommended.

First prepare the build system if necessary:

    autoreconf -fi

Then create and enter a build directory:

    mkdir -p build/autotools-linux
    cd build/autotools-linux

Configure using the source tree:

    ../../configure

Compile:

    make -j$(nproc)

Optionally run the supplied tests:

    make check

Install using the usual target:

    make install

The installation prefix and other build options may be selected using
the normal configure options.  Run:

    ../../configure --help

for the available options.


Distribution Testing
====================

Maintainers should use:

    make distcheck

before preparing a source release.

distcheck creates a source archive and verifies that the distributed
source can be configured, built, tested, installed, and uninstalled
without depending on files omitted from the distribution.


Build Tree Notes
================

The Autotools and CMake builds use separate build directories under
build/.  This permits multiple build configurations to coexist.

CMake generates its own config.h in the build tree.  The CMake build is
arranged so that this generated configuration takes precedence over a
config.h that may have been generated in the source tree by Autotools.

The current Windows build target is 64-bit Windows.  The former Cygwin
build path and older 32-bit Windows build assumptions are no longer
supported.
